WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.874
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.174 --> 00:00:08.770
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:15.263
Unit nine: Peer Review Process. Today you will write concisely with reduced relative clauses.

4
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:18.204
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.214
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:23.970
Reviewer.

7
00:00:24.983 --> 00:00:29.084
Reviewer means an expert who evaluates a submission before publication.

8
00:00:29.683 --> 00:00:33.506
One reviewer recommended rejection; the other, minor revisions.

9
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:37.013
Your turn. Say: Reviewer.

10
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:40.717
Reviewer.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:42.589
Anonymity.

12
00:00:43.583 --> 00:00:47.313
Anonymity means the condition of having one's identity concealed.

13
00:00:47.917 --> 00:00:51.426
Anonymity allows junior scholars to criticise senior ones.

14
00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:54.634
Your turn. Say: Anonymity.

15
00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:58.640
Anonymity.

16
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:00.233
Bias.

17
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:04.620
Bias means a systematic tendency that distorts judgement.

18
00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:08.842
Reviewers may display bias towards prestigious institutions.

19
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.137
Your turn. Say: Bias.

20
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.780
Bias.

21
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:17.704
Methodology.

22
00:01:18.717 --> 00:01:22.342
Methodology means the overall design and procedures of a study.

23
00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.378
The methodology adopted in the paper was unconventional.

24
00:01:26.983 --> 00:01:29.729
Your turn. Say: Methodology.

25
00:01:32.567 --> 00:01:33.661
Methodology.

26
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:35.220
Critique.

27
00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:41.107
Critique means a reasoned evaluation identifying strengths and weaknesses.

28
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:45.179
A constructive critique suggests remedies, not just flaws.

29
00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:48.424
Your turn. Say: Critique.

30
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:52.140
Critique.

31
00:01:52.883 --> 00:01:54.244
Revision cycle.

32
00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:59.305
Revision cycle means one round of reviewer comments and author amendments.

33
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:02.601
The paper went through three revision cycles in a year.

34
00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:06.566
Your turn. Say: Revision cycle.

35
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.807
Revision cycle.

36
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:12.725
Blind review.

37
00:02:13.717 --> 00:02:17.075
Blind review means review in which identities are concealed.

38
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:20.832
In double-blind review, neither side knows the other.

39
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:24.197
Your turn. Say: Blind review.

40
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:28.568
Blind review.

41
00:02:29.317 --> 00:02:30.480
Integrity.

42
00:02:31.483 --> 00:02:35.015
Integrity means adherence to honest and ethical standards.

43
00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:38.858
Undisclosed conflicts of interest threaten integrity.

44
00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.079
Your turn. Say: Integrity.

45
00:02:44.933 --> 00:02:46.190
Integrity.

46
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.802
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:56.613
Visal, you've just finished your first peer review for the student journal. What stood out?

48
00:02:57.717 --> 00:03:07.289
Frankly, the methodology used in the study raised questions. The sample, drawn from a single class, was far too small.

49
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:10.278
Did you recommend rejection?

50
00:03:11.383 --> 00:03:19.016
No. I suggested major revisions, explaining how a sample taken from several schools would improve validity.

51
00:03:20.117 --> 00:03:21.965
Was it difficult to be critical?

52
00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:29.234
A little. But the review being blind helped. I could focus on the arguments presented rather than on who wrote them.

53
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:31.831
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:32.683 --> 00:03:35.577
Grammar focus: Reduced relative clauses.

55
00:03:35.877 --> 00:03:37.052
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.980
The methodology used in the study raised questions.

57
00:03:41.883 --> 00:03:45.055
The sample, drawn from a single class, was too small.

58
00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:48.948
Reviewers working anonymously tend to be more candid.

59
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:58.903
Drop the relative pronoun and 'be': 'which was used' → 'used' (passive), 'who are working' → 'working' (active). This makes academic prose tighter.

60
00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.322
Be careful! Don't say: The method using in the study was flawed.

61
00:04:06.267 --> 00:04:09.032
Say: The method used in the study was flawed.

62
00:04:09.332 --> 00:04:12.702
A reduced passive clause uses the past participle.

63
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.164
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:16.967 --> 00:04:18.351
Judged by Peers.

65
00:04:18.651 --> 00:04:23.634
Peer review rests on the assumption that experts can evaluate one another's work impartially.

66
00:04:24.633 --> 00:04:30.557
In practice, reviewers working under anonymity are expected to judge arguments rather than reputations.

67
00:04:31.567 --> 00:04:38.744
Typically, the methodology used in the study attracts the most scrutiny, since flawed methods invalidate any conclusion.

68
00:04:39.733 --> 00:04:45.541
Critics argue, however, that reviewers familiar with a field can often guess an author's identity.

69
00:04:46.533 --> 00:04:51.122
Despite such limitations, blind review remains the cornerstone of research integrity.

70
00:04:52.233 --> 00:04:56.688
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.397
The methodology blank in the study raised questions.

72
00:05:00.697 --> 00:05:04.676
A, using. B, used. C, was used.

73
00:05:08.233 --> 00:05:10.279
The answer is B: used.

74
00:05:11.417 --> 00:05:15.021
Reduce: 'Reviewers who work anonymously are more candid.'

75
00:05:15.321 --> 00:05:24.385
A, Reviewers worked anonymously are more candid.. B, Reviewers working anonymously are more candid.. C, Reviewers who working anonymously are more candid..

76
00:05:27.933 --> 00:05:31.999
The answer is B: Reviewers working anonymously are more candid..

77
00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:37.448
When neither author nor reviewer knows the other's identity, it is blank review.

78
00:05:37.748 --> 00:05:41.286
A, double-blind. B, open. C, single.

79
00:05:44.833 --> 00:05:47.216
The answer is A: double-blind.

80
00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.021
A blank critique suggests remedies, not just flaws.

81
00:05:52.321 --> 00:05:56.637
A, constructive. B, destructive. C, fallacious.

82
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.943
The answer is A: constructive.

83
00:06:04.083 --> 00:06:07.999
According to the reading, what limitation of blind review do critics mention?

84
00:06:08.299 --> 00:06:14.669
A, Reviewers can often guess the author.. B, It is too fast.. C, It ignores methodology..

85
00:06:18.233 --> 00:06:21.649
The answer is A: Reviewers can often guess the author..

86
00:06:22.833 --> 00:06:26.136
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:26.983 --> 00:06:30.225
What are the benefits and drawbacks of anonymous peer review?

88
00:06:47.017 --> 00:06:48.354
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:48.654 --> 00:07:22.929
The main benefit of anonymous review is that work is judged on its merits, not on the reputation of the author. It also allows junior researchers to criticise senior colleagues without fear. For example, a weakness identified by a young reviewer might otherwise go unmentioned. On the other hand, anonymity can encourage harsh or careless comments, since reviewers are not accountable. On balance, I think anonymity is worth keeping, provided editors monitor the tone of reviews.

90
00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:24.810
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:25.717 --> 00:07:30.447
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and reduced relative clauses.

92
00:07:30.747 --> 00:07:35.199
Today's value is fairness. Look for solutions that are fair to everyone.

93
00:07:35.499 --> 00:07:37.931
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:38.231 --> 00:07:41.022
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

